🛠️ Basic Build Commands
- gradle build — Compiles and builds the entire project.
- gradle clean — Deletes the build directory.
- gradle assemble — Assembles outputs without running tests.
- gradle check — Runs all verification tasks (e.g., tests).
- gradle test — Executes unit tests.
- gradle run — Runs the main class (if using the application plugin).
- gradle javadoc — Generates Javadoc documentation.

📋 Task Management
- gradle tasks — Lists available tasks.
- gradle tasks --all — Lists all tasks including those from plugins.
- gradle dependencies — Displays project dependencies.
- gradle properties — Shows project properties.

⚙️ Project Setup & Configuration
- gradle init — Initializes a new Gradle project.
- gradle wrapper — Generates Gradle wrapper scripts.
- gradle help — Displays help information.

🧪 Testing & Verification
- gradle test — Runs tests.
- gradle check — Runs all checks (tests, lint, etc.).
- gradle build --scan — Creates a build scan for diagnostics.

🚀 Advanced & Continuous Builds
- gradle build --continuous — Watches for changes and rebuilds automatically.
- gradle --daemon — Uses the Gradle daemon for faster builds.
- gradle --offline — Builds without accessing the network.

🧩 Plugin & Dependency Management
- gradle dependencyInsight --dependency <name> — Shows details about a specific dependency.
- gradle buildEnvironment — Displays build script dependencies.

🧵 Kotlin-Specific Enhancements
If you're using Kotlin DSL (build.gradle.kts), all these commands still apply. You can also:
- Use kotlin("jvm") version "x.y.z" in your plugins block.
- Run Kotlin compilation tasks like compileKotlin and compileTestKotlin.

You can find more details in the Gradle CLI documentation or explore this Gradle Commands Cheat Sheet.
